---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import WritingHero from '../../components/writing/WritingHero.astro';
import WritingArchive from '../../components/writing/WritingArchive.astro';
import { showDrafts } from '../../lib/show-drafts';
import { markdownSiblingFor } from '../../lib/markdown-negotiation.mjs';

// The page's markdown sibling, declared in the head by Layout. Derived from the negotiation map
// rather than written out, so this link and the sibling the middleware would actually serve
// cannot drift; the map returns null for a path it does not negotiate, and Layout wants the prop
// absent rather than null.
const markdownUrl = markdownSiblingFor('/writing/') ?? undefined;

const allArticles = await getCollection('writing', ({ data }) => showDrafts || !data.draft);

const articles = allArticles.sort((a, b) => b.data.date.getTime() - a.data.date.getTime());
---

<Layout
  title="Writing on growth, marketing, AI, and building"
  description="Essays on growth, marketing, AI, and building software as a non-engineer, from the Director of Growth at Temporal Technologies."
  markdownAlternate={markdownUrl}
>
  {/*
    ONE INDEX, styled by both appearances.

    The hero and the archive below are the page's whole body. Retro restyles this
    same markup under `html[data-appearance='retro'] [data-module='writing-…']`
    in src/styles/retro.css — the legacy PageHeader/ArticleList tree and the
    `.retro-skin` wrapper that shipped a second copy of the index are deleted.

    Every retro rule for this body carries a `[data-module]` prefix, and that is
    load-bearing twice over: Astro appends a `[data-astro-cid-…]` to each
    component's own selectors and that attribute counts as a class, and the
    archive's row classes (`.row`, `.row-title`, `.year-group`) are the same
    generic names the homepage and changelog rows use, so an unprefixed rule
    would reach surfaces this page does not own.

    ONE SORT, strictly reverse-chronological. The legacy list floated a
    `featured` post to the top; the archive groups by year, and a year heading
    over a list that is not in date order reads as a bug. With the legacy list
    gone the flag ordered and rendered nothing anywhere, so it was removed from
    the schema and the scaffold on 2026-08-29.

    Tags leave the HTML with that list too, and stay live as data: /llms.txt,
    /llms-full.txt, the WebMCP post search and the A2A digest all read them from
    the collection.
  */}
  <WritingHero />
  <WritingArchive articles={articles} />
</Layout>
